  'rafter-length': {"title":"Rafter Length Calculator: Horizontal Run & Rise","metaDesc":"Enter your horizontal run and rise and Rafter Length Calculator returns a rafter length of 13.00 ft, with every step of the math shown.","canonicalPath":"/construction/rafter-length","cat":"construction","catName":"Construction","lsi":["rafter length calculator roof pitch run rise framing","free online rafter length calculator","rafter length formula","how to calculate rafter length","rafter length formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does this calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator works out the rafter length from the Horizontal Run and Rise, following standard Construction conventions — the page defaults produce a rafter length of 13.00 ft.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Horizontal Run and Rise.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the rafter length, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the published Construction relationship between the Horizontal Run and Rise and the rafter length.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (horizontal run of 12, rise of 5), rafter length calculator returns a rafter length of 13.00 ft.</p><h2>How does the Rafter Length Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator computes the rafter length directly from your inputs — the Horizontal Run and Rise feed the formula. Your numbers stay in the tab and the rafter length recalculates the moment a field changes.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified output — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Horizontal Run</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Rise</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>The output panel in rafter length calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Explore. Each input change recalculates instantly; watching the rafter length move tells you which factor dominates your case.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>Rafter Length Calculator substitutes the Horizontal Run and Rise into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the rafter length rounded for readability.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with horizontal run of 12, rise of 5, this rafter length calculation returns <strong>Rafter Length: 13.00 ft</strong>. The same run reports Pitch: 5.0/12 — order 10-15% extra for overhangs, ridge cuts, and waste.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: Rafter = √(run² + rise²) — the Pythagorean theorem</li><li>12² + 5² = 144 + 25</li><li>√169 = 13.00 ft</li><li>Add the overhang measured along the same slope, not horizontally</li></ul>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from rafter length calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Rafter Length Calculator: planning around a target figure, checking a what-if case, and checking the arithmetic without a spreadsheet. Because every operation in Rafter Length Calculator is listed, the page works as a cross-check on a figure you were handed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The classic error when using rafter length calculator is a mismatched unit or scale, and the steps panel surfaces it faster than re-reading the inputs.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Rafter Length Calculator returns Rafter Length: 13.00 ft at its defaults; double the Horizontal Run (ft) and it returns Rafter Length: 24.52 ft.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Rafter Length Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because it is fast and private — Rafter Length Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does this calculator do?","a":"Use Rafter Length Calculator when the rafter length needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Construction method and shows the working, not just the answer. Because the page doubles as documentation: Rafter Length Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: rafter = √(run² + rise²) — the pythagorean theorem, then 12² + 5² = 144 + 25. The engine behind Rafter Length Calculator evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the figure you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Rafter Length Calculator?","a":"Rafter Length Calculator needs Horizontal Run and Rise and nothing else."},{"q":"What happens if I double the Horizontal Run (ft)?","a":"At the defaults Rafter Length Calculator returns Rafter Length: 13.00 ft; doubling Horizontal Run (ft) to 24 moves it to Rafter Length: 24.52 ft."},{"q":"What does the result from this calculator mean?","a":"The main number the rafter length calculator returns is the rafter length for your exact inputs. The model behind Rafter Length Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Students, planners, and professionals use it for planning around a target figure, checking a what-if case, and checking the arithmetic without a spreadsheet, and for sanity-checking numbers that arrived from somewhere else. Run Rafter Length Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the output is, which a single run never shows."}]},
