  'moon-phase': {"title":"Moon Phase Calculator: Tonight's Phase and Illumination","metaDesc":"Find the moon phase for any date, plus the illumination percentage and the days to the next new or full moon, worked out from the lunar cycle.","canonicalPath":"/everyday/moon-phase","cat":"everyday","catName":"Everyday Life","lsi":["moon phase calculator lunar cycle synodic month full moon","free online moon phase calculator","moon phase formula","how to calculate moon phase","lunar phase formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What phase is the moon tonight?</h2>\n<p>The moon runs a 29.53-day cycle from new moon to new moon, and any date can be placed on it. The page reports the phase name, the illuminated fraction and the days to the next new or full moon, calculated from the cycle rather than read off a table.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Date.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the lunar phase, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Everyday Life method for the lunar phase, run locally in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>A full moon is 100% illuminated around day 14 or 15 of the cycle, and a new moon is 0% and rises with the sun.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Moon Phase Calculator computes the lunar phase directly from your inputs — the Date feed the formula. The lunar phase is calculated in the browser, so the inputs never leave the device.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Moon Phase Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Use Moon Phase Calculator when the output needs to be right the first time: it evaluates your inputs against the standard Everyday Life method and shows the working, not just the answer.</p>\n<h2>Using the Moon Phase Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Date</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>The output panel in moon phase calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the result shows which lever matters most for your moon phase question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The engine behind Moon Phase Calculator evaluates the inputs in a single pass — no hidden iterations or adjustments — so the figure you see is exactly what the formula produces for the values you entered.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with date of 2000-01-06, this moon phase calculation returns <strong>New Moon</strong>. The same run reports Lunar age: 0.00 days into the 29.53-day synodic month | Illumination: 0.0% (approximate ±1 day).</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Method: count days since a known new moon (2000-01-06 18:14 UTC), then take the remainder over the 29.53-day synodic month</li><li>Age = 0.00 days</li><li>Match to the 8-phase cycle (new → first quarter → full → last quarter)</li><li>Approximation drifts about a day over decades — almanac values are authoritative for observation</li></ul>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from moon phase calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Moon Phase Calculator: day-to-day planning, trying the same numbers twice, and double-checking a figure before acting on it. Because every operation in Moon Phase Calculator is listed, the page works as a cross-check on a figure you were handed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>The classic error when using moon phase calculator is a mismatched unit or scale, and the steps panel surfaces it faster than re-reading the inputs.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> A useful habit with moon phase calculator: vary one input at a time and note how far the lunar phase moves before trusting any single run.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Moon Phase Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the working is visible: Moon Phase Calculator shows each operation behind the result in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Moon Phase Calculator calculate?","a":"Moon Phase Calculator answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the lunar phase? Enter the Date, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath. Because it is fast and private — Moon Phase Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed."},{"q":"How is the lunar phase calculated?","a":"The first steps are method: count days since a known new moon (2000-01-06 18:14 utc), then take the remainder over the 29.53-day synodic month, then age = 0.00 days. The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Moon Phase Calculator makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the result is hidden."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Moon Phase Calculator?","a":"Moon Phase Calculator needs Date and nothing else."},{"q":"What does the result from the Moon Phase Calculator mean?","a":"The main number the moon phase calculator returns is the lunar phase for your exact inputs. The model behind Moon Phase Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the Moon Phase Calculator most useful?","a":"Moon Phase Calculator earns its place in the Everyday Life toolkit wherever the figure has to be explained, not just produced. Run Moon Phase Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows."}]},
