  'gift-wrap': {"title":"How Much Wrapping Paper Do I Need? Box Size Calculator","metaDesc":"Enter the box length, width and height to get the paper area you need for the wrap, with the overlap included so the paper actually covers the box.","canonicalPath":"/everyday/gift-wrap","cat":"everyday","catName":"Everyday Life","lsi":["gift wrap calculator wrapping paper box size area","free online gift wrap calculator","gift wrap formula","how to calculate gift wrap","paper formula"],"aeo":"<h2>How much wrapping paper do I need?</h2>\n<p>Wrapping paper has to cover the four sides of the box plus the overlap that folds underneath and tucks at the ends. That is why a paper area is always larger than the box surface alone, and why a box that looks small can still need a third of a square metre.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Box Length, Box Width, and Box Height.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the paper, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Everyday Life method for the paper, run locally in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>A 30 x 20 x 10 cm box needs about 0.30 sq m of paper with the overlap built in. Buy the next size sheet up for an awkward shape or a deep fold, since running out mid-wrap means starting over.</p><h2>How does the Gift Wrap Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Gift Wrap Calculator computes the paper directly from your inputs — the Box Length, Box Width, and Box Height feed the formula. The paper is calculated in the browser, so the inputs never leave the device.</p>","desc":"<h2>How the Gift Wrap Calculator works</h2>\n<p>Gift Wrap Calculator answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the paper? Enter the Box Length, Box Width, and Box Height, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Box Length</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Box Width</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li><strong>Box Height</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li>Note the paper Gift Wrap Calculator reports. It updates as you type, and the worked steps beneath Gift Wrap Calculator make the arithmetic auditable.</li>\n<li>When rerunning gift wrap calculator, change a single input between attempts — the resulting spread shows which factor actually drives the paper.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Gift Wrap Calculator makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the paper is hidden.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with box length of 40, box width of 30, box height of 15, this gift wrap calculation returns <strong>Paper: 0.63 sq m (6300 sq cm)</strong>. The same run reports Formula covers wrap-over and tuck ends — add 10% for patterned paper where you cannot rotate pieces.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: Area = 2(W + H) × (L + 2H)</li><li>2(30 + 15) = 90 cm wide strip</li><li>(40 + 2×15) = 70 cm long</li><li>Area = 90 × 70 = 6300 sq cm</li></ul>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from gift wrap calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Common scenarios for Gift Wrap Calculator: planning ahead, testing a second scenario, and double-checking a figure before acting on it. The visible working is what makes Gift Wrap Calculator a cross-check rather than a black box.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Mixing up inputs with similar labels is the classic gift wrap mistake; the steps panel is the quickest way to spot a value that landed in the wrong field.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Gift Wrap Calculator returns Paper: 0.63 sq m (6300 sq cm) at its defaults; double the Box Length (cm) and it returns Paper: 0.99 sq m (9900 sq cm).</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Results from Gift Wrap Calculator are estimates computed from the values entered; real-world outcomes can differ when fees, taxes, or conditions not modeled here apply.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Choosing gift wrap calculator comes down to transparency — the working stays visible, the math stays local, and the result is reproducible line by line.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does this calculator do?","a":"Every run of Gift Wrap Calculator evaluates the Box Length, Box Width, and Box Height you enter, applies the standard Everyday Life formula, and reports the paper with each step listed for review. Because the paper arrives with supporting figures and a full step list, the page gives you context rather than a single bare number."},{"q":"How is the paper calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: area = 2(w + h) × (l + 2h), then 2(30 + 15) = 90 cm wide strip. The formula section on this page spells out how gift wrap calculator turns the Box Length, Box Width, and Box Height into the paper, one operation at a time."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Gift Wrap Calculator?","a":"Gift Wrap Calculator needs Box Length, Box Width, and Box Height and nothing else."},{"q":"What happens if I double the Box Length (cm)?","a":"At the defaults Gift Wrap Calculator returns Paper: 0.63 sq m (6300 sq cm); doubling Box Length (cm) to 80 moves it to Paper: 0.99 sq m (9900 sq cm)."},{"q":"What does the result from this calculator mean?","a":"The main number the gift wrap calculator returns is the paper for your exact inputs. Gift Wrap Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the output proportionally."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Gift Wrap Calculator include planning ahead, testing a second scenario, and double-checking a figure before acting on it — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. In practice, a useful habit with gift wrap calculator: vary one input at a time and note how far the paper moves before trusting any single run."}]},
