  'free-fall': {"title":"Falling Object Calculator: Free Fall Speed & Rate","metaDesc":"Free Fall Calculator takes your height and gravity and returns a line-by-line breakdown, with the working shown beside it. Use it offline after the first visit.","canonicalPath":"/science/free-fall","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["free fall calculator","free online free fall calculator","fall formula","how to calculate fall","time formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does this calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Free Fall Calculator works out the time from the Height and Gravity, following standard Science conventions — the page defaults produce a time of 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Height and Gravity.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the time, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Science formula behind the time, evaluated entirely in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (height of 100, gravity of 9.8), free fall calculator returns a time of 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Free Fall Calculator computes the time directly from your inputs — the Height and Gravity feed the formula. Nothing is uploaded: the time is computed locally and appears as you type.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Free Fall Calculator keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Height and Gravity, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line.</p>\n<h2>Using the Free Fall Calculator</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Height</strong> — in free fall calculator, this value feeds the formula directly, and the steps panel shows exactly where it enters the time.</li>\n<li><strong>Gravity</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>Review the output. Beyond the headline time, the intermediate steps are listed — useful for catching a mistyped input.</li>\n<li>Iterate. Vary the inputs one at a time; the movement in the time shows which lever matters most for your free fall question.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The calculation in Free Fall Calculator applies the standard Science method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with height of 100, gravity of 9.8, this free fall calculation returns <strong>Time: 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s</strong>. The same run reports h = ½gt².</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: h = ½gt² → t = √(2h/g)</li><li>t = √(2 × 100 / 9.8) = √20.41</li><li>t = 4.52 s</li><li>vf = g × t = 9.8 × 4.52 = 44.27 m/s</li></ul>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from free fall calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for Free Fall Calculator include planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the time — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Rounding intermediate values by hand introduces error Free Fall Calculator does not have; it keeps full precision internally, so trust the displayed output over mental arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Free Fall Calculator returns Time: 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s at its defaults; double the Height (m) and it returns Time: 6.39s | Final Speed: 62.61 m/s.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The model behind Free Fall Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because the page doubles as documentation: Free Fall Calculator puts the formula, a worked example, and the assumptions right beside the calculator.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does this calculator do?","a":"Free Fall Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified result — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it. Because the working is visible: Free Fall Calculator shows each operation behind the time in the steps panel, so you can verify the result instead of trusting a black box."},{"q":"How is the time calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: h = ½gt² → t = √(2h/g), then t = √(2 × 100 / 9.8) = √20.41. Free Fall Calculator substitutes the Height and Gravity into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the figure rounded for readability."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Free Fall Calculator?","a":"Free Fall Calculator needs Height and Gravity and nothing else."},{"q":"What happens if I double the Height (m)?","a":"At the defaults Free Fall Calculator returns Time: 4.52s | Final Speed: 44.27 m/s; doubling Height (m) to 200 moves it to Time: 6.39s | Final Speed: 62.61 m/s."},{"q":"What does the result from this calculator mean?","a":"The main number the free fall calculator returns is the time for your exact inputs. The time is only as complete as the inputs: anything the page does not ask for (fees, variability, local rules) sits outside the calculation."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Common scenarios for Free Fall Calculator: planning around a target figure, comparing scenarios side by side, and double-checking the time. Read the steps Free Fall Calculator prints once and the same output can be reproduced by hand. Run Free Fall Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the result is, which a single run never shows."}]},
