  'dew-point': {"title":"Dew Point Calculator: When Will Condensation Form?","metaDesc":"Enter air temperature and relative humidity to get the dew point with the Magnus formula. At 30 °C and 70% humidity it is 23.9 °C, where condensation starts.","canonicalPath":"/science/dew-point","cat":"science","catName":"Science","lsi":["dew point calculator humidity temperature magnus formula condensation","dew point formula","how to calculate dew point","dew point formula"],"aeo":"<h2>How is dew point calculated?</h2>\n<p>Dew point is the temperature the air must cool to before water vapour begins to condense. It comes from two inputs — air temperature and relative humidity — run through the Magnus formula, not from a lookup table.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Air Temperature and Relative Humidity.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the dew point, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> standard Science arithmetic for the dew point, computed on the device.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>At 30 °C and 70% relative humidity the dew point is 23.9 °C: once a surface drops to that temperature, condensation starts on it.</p><h2>How does the Dew Point Calculator work?</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator computes the dew point directly from your inputs — the Air Temperature and Relative Humidity feed the formula. The dew point is derived locally, which is why the page still works after the connection drops.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Dew Point Calculator answers one question well — given the values you provide, what is the output? Enter the Air Temperature and Relative Humidity, and the result panel returns the value with the full working underneath.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Air Temperature</strong> — the value that feeds directly into the formula — match it to the scenario you are modeling before moving on.</li>\n<li><strong>Relative Humidity</strong> — a core input the formula applies directly — keep the units consistent with the label.</li>\n<li>The output panel in dew point calculator leads with the headline result and follows with the steps behind it, so the value can be checked rather than assumed.</li>\n<li>When rerunning dew point calculator, change a single input between attempts — the resulting spread shows which factor actually drives the dew point.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The relationship between the inputs is fixed by the formula, and Dew Point Calculator makes each substitution explicit so nothing about the dew point is hidden.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with air temperature of 30, relative humidity of 70, this dew point calculation returns <strong>23.9 °C</strong>. The same run reports Magnus formula | Humidex comfort: below 13 °C dry, 16–21 °C comfortable, above 21 °C muggy.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>alpha = ln(RH/100) + (17.27×T)/(237.7+T) = 1.579</li><li>Dew point = (237.7×alpha)/(17.27−alpha)</li></ul>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>To interpret the result from dew point calculator, read it together with the intermediate figures — the pairing is what makes the number auditable.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Typical uses for Dew Point Calculator include day-to-day planning, trying the same numbers twice, and confirming a total before you commit — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Rounding intermediate values by hand introduces error Dew Point Calculator does not have; it keeps full precision internally, so trust the displayed dew point over mental arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Dew Point Calculator returns 23.9 °C at its defaults; double the Air Temperature (°C) and it returns 52.5 °C.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Treat the dew point as a planning figure rather than a binding quote, and confirm important decisions with the relevant professional.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Because it is fast and private — Dew Point Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does the Dew Point Calculator calculate?","a":"Every run of Dew Point Calculator evaluates the Air Temperature and Relative Humidity you enter, applies the standard Science formula, and reports the result with each step listed for review. Choosing dew point calculator comes down to transparency — the working stays visible, the math stays local, and the result is reproducible line by line."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are alpha = ln(rh/100) + (17.27×t)/(237.7+t) = 1.579, then dew point = (237.7×alpha)/(17.27−alpha). The formula operates on the values exactly as entered; keeping the units shown in each label is what makes the dew point trustworthy."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Dew Point Calculator?","a":"Dew Point Calculator needs Air Temperature and Relative Humidity and nothing else."},{"q":"What happens if I double the Air Temperature (°C)?","a":"At the defaults Dew Point Calculator returns 23.9 °C; doubling Air Temperature (°C) to 60 moves it to 52.5 °C."},{"q":"What does the result from the Dew Point Calculator mean?","a":"The main number the dew point calculator returns is the dew point for your exact inputs. The assumptions listed for dew point calculator bound the estimate: inputs outside the labeled ranges can make the dew point mathematically valid but practically implausible."},{"q":"When is the Dew Point Calculator most useful?","a":"Dew Point Calculator fits planning and checking: day-to-day planning, trying the same numbers twice, and confirming a total before you commit, or any moment when the figure needs to be right the first time. Run Dew Point Calculator twice with deliberately low and high inputs; the spread tells you how sensitive the figure is, which a single run never shows."}]},
