  'password-entropy': {"title":"Password Entropy Calculator: Password Length","metaDesc":"Password Entropy Calculator computes an entropy of 71.5 bits from your password length and character pool size and shows each step of the calculation.","canonicalPath":"/utilities/password-entropy","cat":"utilities","catName":"Utilities","lsi":["password entropy calculator bits strength character pool brute force","free online password entropy calculator","password entropy formula","how to calculate password entropy","entropy formula"],"aeo":"<h2>What does this calculator do?</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator works out the entropy from the Password Length and Character Pool Size, following standard Utilities conventions — the page defaults produce an entropy of 71.5 bits.</p><ul><li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Password Length and Character Pool Size.</li><li><strong>Output:</strong> the entropy, plus the intermediate steps behind it.</li><li><strong>Method:</strong> the standard Utilities method for the entropy, run locally in your browser.</li></ul><h3>Quick answer</h3>\n<p>With the default inputs (password length of 12, character pool size of 62), password entropy calculator returns an entropy of 71.5 bits.</p><h2>How does it work?</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator computes the entropy directly from your inputs — the Password Length and Character Pool Size feed the formula. The entropy is calculated in the browser, so the inputs never leave the device.</p>","desc":"<h2>How it works</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator keeps the whole calculation in front of you — the Password Length and Character Pool Size, the formula, the intermediate steps, and a worked example you can reproduce line by line.</p>\n<h2>How to use it</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Password Length</strong> — one of the values the calculation builds from; the result reflects exactly what you type here.</li>\n<li><strong>Character Pool Size</strong> — used in the first stage of the calculation, so entering it accurately matters more than any later refinement.</li>\n<li>Note the figure Password Entropy Calculator reports. It updates as you type, and the worked steps beneath Password Entropy Calculator make the arithmetic auditable.</li>\n<li>When rerunning password entropy calculator, change a single input between attempts — the resulting spread shows which factor actually drives the entropy.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>The formula behind the result</h2>\n<p>The calculation in Password Entropy Calculator applies the standard Utilities method, keeping full precision internally and rounding only the final display.</p>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> with password length of 12, character pool size of 62, this password entropy calculation returns <strong>Entropy: 71.5 bits</strong>. The same run reports Pool^length = 62^12 | Median crack time at 10¹² guesses/s: 51 years — reasonable | Assumes truly random characters;.</p><p>The steps it follows:</p><ul><li>Formula: entropy = length × log₂(pool size)</li><li>log₂(62) = 5.9542</li><li>bits = 12 × 5.9542 = 71.5</li><li>Each extra character multiplies search space by the pool size — length beats complexity</li></ul>\n<h2>Understanding the result</h2>\n<p>The entropy is the headline answer; the supporting figures beneath it and the step list give the surrounding context needed to judge it.</p>\n<h2>Where it helps</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator fits planning and checking: short-term planning, testing a second scenario, and spot-checking a quoted figure, or any moment when the figure needs to be right the first time.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p>Mixing up inputs with similar labels is the classic password entropy mistake; the steps panel is the quickest way to spot a value that landed in the wrong field.</p>\n<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Password Entropy Calculator returns Entropy: 71.5 bits at its defaults; double the Password Length and it returns Entropy: 142.9 bits.</p>\n<h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>Password Entropy Calculator assumes the units shown in each label — entering values in different units will skew the result proportionally.</p>\n<h2>Why use this calculator</h2>\n<p>Choosing password entropy calculator comes down to transparency — the working stays visible, the math stays local, and the result is reproducible line by line.</p>","faqs":[{"q":"What does this calculator do?","a":"Password Entropy Calculator turns the values you enter into a verified entropy — the formula, every intermediate step, and the assumptions sit beside the result instead of hidden behind it. Because it is fast and private — Password Entropy Calculator runs entirely in your browser, nothing is uploaded, and no account is needed."},{"q":"How is the result calculated?","a":"The first steps are formula: entropy = length × log₂(pool size), then log₂(62) = 5.9542. Password Entropy Calculator substitutes the Password Length and Character Pool Size into the formula, evaluates it in the order shown in the steps panel, and reports the output rounded for readability."},{"q":"What do I need to use the Password Entropy Calculator?","a":"Password Entropy Calculator needs Password Length and Character Pool Size and nothing else."},{"q":"What happens if I double the Password Length?","a":"At the defaults Password Entropy Calculator returns Entropy: 71.5 bits; doubling Password Length to 24 moves it to Entropy: 142.9 bits."},{"q":"What does the result from this calculator mean?","a":"The main number the password entropy calculator returns is the entropy for your exact inputs. The model behind Password Entropy Calculator covers the standard case; special cases, edge values, or jurisdiction-specific rules may need manual adjustment."},{"q":"When is the page most useful?","a":"Typical uses for Password Entropy Calculator include short-term planning, testing a second scenario, and spot-checking a quoted figure — anywhere the figure needs to be defensible rather than guessed. If the entropy looks wrong, read the steps panel before re-entering anything; it usually shows exactly where the number departed from expectation."}]},
